# Copy to .env and fill in.
#
# These differ in reach. EXPO_PUBLIC_DEV_TOKEN is behind __DEV__, so it folds to
# null in a release bundle. EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL and
# EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_IOS_CLIENT_ID are NOT — they are read in every build, and
# every EXPO_PUBLIC_* value is inlined into the binary in clear text. Nothing
# secret can go in this file.

# Which RunGuard backend this build talks to. Read ONCE, at bundle time —
# src/lib/api.ts does `process.env.EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL || "http://localhost:3000"`
# as a module-level const, so there is no runtime switch and no in-app field to
# change it. The value you build with is the value that ships.
#
#   local dev, iOS Simulator   http://localhost:3000   (shares the Mac's loopback)
#   local dev, physical phone  http://<your Mac's LAN IP>:3000
#   testing against Preview    the Vercel preview URL   → the DEV database
#   a release build            the production URL       → the PROD database
#
# A RELEASE BUILD MUST NOT RELY ON THE FALLBACK. Unset, it ships
# http://localhost:3000, which on a phone is the phone — every request fails.
# There is no health endpoint, so it does not error: it hangs for 15 seconds and
# then reports that the server is unreachable, on every screen, forever.
EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=

# Skips the login screen in dev: src/lib/auth.ts sends this as the Bearer when
# nothing is in the keychain. It is a SESSION TOKEN, obtained by signing in —
# the same value a real sign-in stores, and a full credential for whoever it
# names. Sign in as your own seeded athlete or team (`npm run seed` in website/)
# to get one, and never reuse someone else's.
#
# FORMAT CHANGED — a token you saved before password reset landed is DEAD. Session
# tokens now carry a version (`a.<id>.<version>.<hmac>`) so that a reset can
# invalidate every token issued before it. auth.ts ignores a token with the wrong
# number of segments rather than sending it, so a stale value here means the app
# opens at the login screen instead of failing on every screen with a 401. Sign in
# normally to get a current one.
#
# EITHER ROLE. `auth.ts` reads which from the token's own shape:
# `a.<id>.<version>.<hmac>` is an athlete, `<teamId>.<version>.<hmac>` is a coach. So switching the simulator
# between the athlete tabs and the coach tabs is editing this one line. One
# variable rather than two on purpose — two would be an athlete and a coach
# credential present at once, which `getCredential()` refuses outright.
EXPO_PUBLIC_DEV_TOKEN=

# The Google OAuth client the app authorizes against — an iOS-type client, whose
# bundle id must be run.runguard.app. Create it in Google Cloud Console; it is
# NOT the same client as the website's GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID.
#
# NOT A SECRET, and that is a property of the client type rather than a shrug.
# Google's iOS clients are *public* clients: they are issued no secret at all,
# because one would have to ship inside the binary where it could be read. What
# actually authenticates the exchange is PKCE, and the code the app receives is
# redeemed by the server (see website/src/app/api/mobile/google/route.ts) so
# that the id_token is never handled on the phone.
#
# ONE VALUE, TWO PLACES. app.json's ios.infoPlist.CFBundleURLTypes must register
# this same client id reversed —
#
#   client id   123456-abc.apps.googleusercontent.com
#   URL scheme  com.googleusercontent.apps.123456-abc
#
# — because Google refuses a redirect to the app's own runguard:// scheme and
# will only send an iOS client back to its reversed id. src/lib/googleSignIn.ts
# derives the redirect from this variable, so the two cannot disagree in code;
# they can still disagree with app.json, and then the sheet closes onto nothing.
# app/ios is gitignored and regenerated, so that entry belongs in app.json and a
# prebuild, never in Info.plist by hand.
#
# Unset, the "Continue with Google" button does not render at all — a button
# that cannot work is worse than no button.
EXPO_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_IOS_CLIENT_ID=
